{
  "cards": [
    {
      "front": "In an ideal DC resistive circuit, what equation relates voltage $V$, current $I$, and resistance $R$, and what are the units of each quantity?",
      "back": "Ohm's law is $V = IR$. Voltage $V$ is measured in volts (V), current $I$ in amperes (A), and resistance $R$ in ohms ($\\Omega$)."
    ,
      "topic": "Ohm's law"
    },
    {
      "front": "Two resistors of $2\\,\\Omega$ and $4\\,\\Omega$ are connected in series to a $12\\,\\text{V}$ source. Use Ohm's law to find the circuit current and the voltage drop across each resistor.",
      "back": "In series, the same current flows through both resistors and the equivalent resistance is $R_{eq}=2+4=6\\,\\Omega$. The current is $I=V/R_{eq}=12/6=2\\,\\text{A}$. The voltage drops are $V_1=IR_1=(2)(2)=4\\,\\text{V}$ and $V_2=IR_2=(2)(4)=8\\,\\text{V}$, which sum to the source voltage."
    ,
      "topic": "Ohm's law"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "cards": [
    {
      "front": "In an ideal DC series resistor circuit, what is the equivalent resistance of resistors $R_1, R_2, \\dots, R_n$ connected in series?",
      "back": "The equivalent resistance is the sum of the individual resistances: $R_{\\text{eq}} = R_1 + R_2 + \\cdots + R_n$. For two series resistors, this is $R_{\\text{eq}} = R_1 + R_2$."
    ,
      "topic": "Series resistor circuits"
    },
    {
      "front": "In an ideal DC series resistor circuit with individual voltage drops $V_1, V_2, \\dots, V_n$ across the resistors, what must the sum of these drops equal?",
      "back": "The sum of the individual voltage drops must equal the source voltage: $V_1 + V_2 + \\cdots + V_n = V_{\\text{source}}$. This follows because the series resistors form a single path and their total resistance is the sum of the individual resistances."
    ,
      "topic": "Series resistor circuits"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "cards": [
    {
      "front": "Two $6\\,\\Omega$ resistors are connected in parallel. Using the reciprocal rule for parallel resistances, what is their equivalent resistance $R_{eq}$?",
      "back": "For parallel branches, the reciprocal equivalent resistance is the sum of the reciprocal branch resistances: $\\frac{1}{R_{eq}}=\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{1}{3}$. Therefore $R_{eq}=3\\,\\Omega$."
    ,
      "topic": "Parallel resistor circuits"
    },
    {
      "front": "An ideal resistor is connected across a fixed voltage $V$. If its resistance $R$ is decreased, how does the power dissipated by the resistor change, and which power expression shows this?",
      "back": "The dissipated power increases. With voltage fixed, use $P=\\frac{V^2}{R}$, so power is inversely proportional to resistance: a smaller $R$ gives a larger $P$."
    ,
      "topic": "Parallel resistor circuits"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "cards": [
    {
      "front": "A resistor network in an ideal DC circuit has total voltage $V$ across it and total current $I$ entering it. What equivalent resistance does the network present to the source?",
      "back": "$R_{eq} = \\dfrac{V}{I}$. The network behaves like a single resistor of value $R_{eq}$ when the same total voltage $V$ produces the same total current $I$."
    ,
      "topic": "Equivalent resistance"
    },
    {
      "front": "For a parallel combination of two or more positive resistors $R_1, R_2, \\dots, R_n$, how does the equivalent resistance $R_{eq}$ compare with each individual branch resistance $R_i$?",
      "back": "$R_{eq}$ is strictly smaller than every branch resistance: $R_{eq} < R_i$ for each $i$. This follows from $\\dfrac{1}{R_{eq}} = \\sum_{j=1}^{n} \\dfrac{1}{R_j}$, so for any branch $i$, $\\dfrac{1}{R_{eq}} > \\dfrac{1}{R_i}$, which implies $R_{eq} < R_i$."
    ,
      "topic": "Equivalent resistance"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "cards": [
    {
      "front": "An ideal resistor carries a fixed current $I$. If its resistance $R$ is increased, how does the power dissipated by the resistor change, and which power expression shows this?",
      "back": "The dissipated power increases. Use $P=I^2R$: with $I$ fixed, $P$ is directly proportional to $R$, so a larger resistance dissipates more power."
    ,
      "topic": "Resistor power dissipation"
    },
    {
      "front": "Two ideal $6\\,\\Omega$ resistors are connected in parallel across a $12\\,\\text{V}$ supply. What is the total power dissipated by the parallel pair?",
      "back": "Each branch has the full $12\\,\\text{V}$, so each dissipates $P=V^2/R=(12\\,\\text{V})^2/(6\\,\\Omega)=24\\,\\text{W}$. The total power is $24\\,\\text{W}+24\\,\\text{W}=48\\,\\text{W}$."
    ,
      "topic": "Resistor power dissipation"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "cards": [
    {
      "front": "In an ideal DC resistive circuit, an ideal ammeter is used to measure the current $i$ through a particular branch. How must the ammeter be connected relative to that branch, and what does that connection imply about the current through the ammeter?",
      "back": "The ideal ammeter is inserted in series with the branch. Because elements in series carry the same current, the current through the ammeter equals the branch current $i$."
    ,
      "topic": "Ideal ammeter and voltmeter connections"
    },
    {
      "front": "In an ideal DC resistive circuit, an ideal voltmeter is used to measure the voltage $v$ across a particular component. How must the voltmeter be connected relative to that component, and what does that connection imply about the voltage across the voltmeter?",
      "back": "The ideal voltmeter is connected in parallel with the component. Because elements in parallel have the same voltage, the voltage across the voltmeter equals the component voltage $v$."
    ,
      "topic": "Ideal ammeter and voltmeter connections"
    }
  ]
}

